  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 1112 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nArea of low pressure currently along the west coast moves \neastward Friday and Saturday, while the western periphery of the \nridge continues over the CWA. A tightening of the pressure \ngradient between these two features will increase southerly low \nlevel flow into the region. Wind gusts of 20 to locally greater \nthan 30 mph are forecast for much of the CWA each afternoon Friday\nand Saturday. These conditions, combined with continued above \nseasonal average temperatures, will help to create at least \nlimited fire weather danger each afternoon with locally enhanced \ngrassland fire spread rates for northeast Oklahoma. \n\nA trailing portion of the low pressure system is progged to drop\ninto the Oklahoma Texas Panhandles Saturday afternoon, and then\nmove across the CWA Saturday night and Sunday. An associated cold\nfront also is forecast to spread east/southeast with the low\npressure system, reaching northeast Oklahoma Saturday night and\nexiting southeast Oklahoma/western Arkansas mid/late morning\nSunday. As the boundary approaches, increasing frontogenetic\nforcing looks to increase along and out ahead of the front to \nprovide enough lift to develop shower/thunderstorm chances. \n\nOverall marginal instability/mid-level lapse rates are forecast \nwhich will help to limit thunderstorm potentials as precip moves \ninto the CWA. The higher potential exists just west of the CWA \nSaturday afternoon/evening, and should weaken with eastward extent\nSaturday night over the CWA. By late Saturday night into Sunday\nmorning, rain chances continue to spread eastward with thunder\nprobs generally less than 15 percent. Rain chances taper off from\nwest to east through the morning hours and exit behind the front\nby early Sunday afternoon. Precipitable water values in excess of\n1 inch could create a brief period of moderate rain Saturday\nnight, though overall rainfall amounts of less than a half inch \nare generally forecast for this event.\n\nIn the wake of the front, temperature are not anticipated to warm\nall that much with highs mainly in the 50s Sunday. Northerly flow\ntransporting a cooler airmass into the region is expected to hold\ninto the first half of next week. Cloud cover is forecast to \nscatter out behind the exiting upper level trof axis Sunday night \nwhich drops low temperatures to/slightly below freezing across \nthe northern half of the CWA. Another cold night with temperatures\nnear/below freezing are forecast Monday night for much of the \nCWA.\n\nGoing to the middle part of next week, latest model solutions\ndiffer with the track, strength, and timing of a shortwave\nforecast to move across the southern/central Plains. Trends with\nthis wave have come in colder aloft and a little more south in the\ntrack compared to 24-hrs ago. Moisture looks to be lacking for\nthis wave to work with. Thus, will keep PoPs below mentionable\ncriteria. However, will continue to monitor with latest data due\nto the potential cold temperature profile.",
